This vignette is a standalone reference for preparing forest-inventory
data into FINN. It documents the inputs of FINN: columns names and units, and
the helper functions for data preparation `makeObsData()` → `resolveSiteIDs()` → `makeInitCohorts()`.

```{r libraries}
library(FINN)
library(data.table)
```

## What FINN needs

FINN is calibrated against clearly defined inputs:

- **`obs_dt`**: demographic responses per site × year × species:
  `siteID, year, ba, dbh, trees, growth, mort, n_at_risk, n_died, reg, species,
  species_name, period_length`. FINN simulates the forest in **yearly steps**,
  so `year` counts years since the first inventory, which is the initial state
  (year 0) and *not* included; with inventories ten years apart the
  observations sit at years 10 and 20, each with `period_length = 10`, the
  years since the previous inventory. NAs are allowed in any response. Units:
  `ba` m², `dbh` cm, `trees` count, `growth` relative growth rate per year,
  `reg` trees ha⁻¹ over the interval.
  Mortality comes back as a **pair of counts**, `n_at_risk` (trees alive at the
  start of the interval) and `n_died` (how many of them were dead at the end),
  with the yearly rate `mort = 1 - (1 - n_died / n_at_risk)^(1 / period_length)`
  derived from them. The pair is the `cbind(died, survived)` response of a
  binomial GLM: the default `loss_family = c(..., mortality = "binomial", ...)`
  weights each observation by `n_at_risk`, so a 60-tree observation outweighs a
  1-tree one.
- **`env_dt`**: `siteID, year, <env vars…>` in **natural units**, one row per
  site and simulated year, from year 1 to the last inventory. No manual
  scaling is needed: FINN z-standardizes the predictors internally when
  fitting (`env_autoscale = TRUE`, the default) and reuses the same constants
  at prediction time.
- **`init_trees`**: the initial state used to build the starting cohorts:
  `siteID, patchID, year, species, dbh, trees, living`.
- **`dist_dt`** (optional): `siteID, year, intensity` (fraction of patches
  disturbed that year).

You assemble these from two raw ingredients: a **tree list** (one row per tree ×
inventory) and a **site environment table**. The rest of this vignette builds the
FINN tables from those two.

## Start from a raw tree list

```{r load-example}
# An example from raw data: Oregon FIA sites, 4 patches each, 3 inventories.
# example_tree_dt.csv and example_env_dt.csv (the climate, loaded later) are both
# built by dev/make_extdata.R from the data-raw/ source; see data-raw/README.md.
tree_dt <- fread(system.file("extdata", "example_tree_dt.csv", package = "FINN"))
str(tree_dt)
```

A raw tree record must include: `siteName, patchName, treeName, year`
(calendar year of the inventory), `species_name`, `dbh` (cm), and a `status` of
`new` / `alive` / `dead`. That is enough: `makeObsData()` reconstructs each tree's
previous state itself, by sorting on `treeName` and `year`. Our example also
contains columns for `status_before` and a `complete` flag marking trees from fully
re-measured plots.

## Acquiring raw data (example: US FIA)

Turning a public inventory into the raw tree list above needs external downloads,
so it is shown but not run. For the US FIA program:

```{r acquire-fia, eval = FALSE}
library(rFIA)
fia <- readFIA(dir = "data/FIA", common = TRUE, states = "OR")
# build tree_dt: dbh = DIA * 2.54 (inch -> cm); status from STATUSCD / PREV_STATUS_CD;
# mort_cause from AGENTCD. See data-raw/fia_raw_dataprep.R in the GitHub repository
```

## Define status flags

FINN's builders key off two logical flags derived from `status`:

```{r status-flags}
# reg    = newly recruited this inventory
# living = currently alive
tree_dt[, reg    := status == "new"]
tree_dt[, living := status %chin% c("alive", "new")]
tree_dt[, .(n = .N), by = .(status, reg, living)]
```

There is deliberately **no tree-level `mort` flag** here. `makeObsData()` derives
mortality itself, from `status` and `living`, as a closed cohort: for each
interval it takes the trees that were alive at the *start*, and counts how many
of them were dead at the *end*. Both columns are pinned to the tree's state at
the start of the interval, which matters because FIA re-identifies a tree's
species between visits; a hand-rolled rate can otherwise book a death against
the species a tree *ended* as while the denominator counted the species it
*started* as, and report more deaths than trees.

## Build `obs_dt` with `makeObsData()`

`makeObsData()` aggregates the tree list to per-patch demographic responses and
lumps rare species into a single `"other"` class. Each argument controls a
data-quality filter:

```{r make-obs}
obs_list <- makeObsData(
  tree_dt           = tree_dt[complete == TRUE],  # only fully re-measured plots
  plotsize          = 0.06,            # ha per patch (scales recruitment to per-ha)
  aggregate_by_site = FALSE,           # keep patch-level resolution
  fix_period_length = 10,              # drop sites whose remeasure gap != 10 yr
  dbh_growth_thresh = c(-10, 50),      # drop sites with implausible dbh change
  NspeciesQuantile  = 0.98,            # keep species covering 98% of stems; rest -> "other"
  minNyears         = 2                # need >= 2 inventories per patch
)
obs_dt <- obs_list$obs_dt
head(obs_dt)
```

Use `Nspecies` (a hard cap) or `NspeciesQuantile` (smallest set covering a stem
fraction) to control how many species you model; everything else becomes
`"other"`, which FINN still tracks as a competitor.

The rates come back **per year**, because FINN simulates the forest in yearly
steps. A tree that grew from 20 to 24 cm over the ten years of an interval has
the yearly growth rate `(24 / 20)^(1 / 10) - 1 = 0.0184`, the constant rate that
compounds to the observed increment, and a cohort of which 1 in 2 trees died
over the interval has the yearly mortality rate `1 - 0.5^(1 / 10) = 0.067`.
`period_length` is the length of the interval in years. When fitting, FINN
averages the simulated yearly growth and mortality over these ten years and sums
the simulated recruits, so `reg` stays the recruitment of the whole interval, and
the counts `n_at_risk`, `n_died` and `growth_n` are the trees behind each
observation. The growth and mortality of every observation, per year and as
compounded back over its interval:

```{r annual-rates}
obs_dt[n_at_risk > 0, .(siteName, year, species_name, period_length, growth, mort,
                        growth_interval = (1 + growth)^period_length - 1,
                        mort_interval   = n_died / n_at_risk)][1:6]
```

`makeObsData(rates = "interval")` returns the rates over the whole interval
instead, for a model in which one simulation step is one census interval (the
set-up of FINN 0.1.0). One step per interval distorts the dynamics, though:
recruitment, competition and growth then act once per interval, and the
mortality limits of a step become limits per decade. Keep the default.

## Environmental data

Acquisition from rasters (e.g. WorldClim) needs external files, so it is shown
but not run. The result is a table of environmental predictors in **natural
units**, one row per site and **calendar year**, because every simulated year
needs its environment:

```{r env-extract, eval = FALSE}
library(terra)
pts  <- vect(site_dt, geom = c("x", "y"), crs = "EPSG:4326")
bio1 <- rast("wc2.1_30s_bio_1.tif")
env_dt[, temp := terra::extract(bio1, project(pts, crs(bio1)))[, 2]]
```

```{r env-load}
env_dt <- fread(system.file("extdata", "example_env_dt.csv", package = "FINN"))
head(env_dt)
env_dt[, .(first = min(year), last = max(year), years = .N), by = siteName]
```

The climate here is the long-term WorldClim normal of each plot, the same in
every year, so it is simply repeated; a yearly climate series would give every
year its own values. `env_dt` must cover every calendar year from the first
inventory of a site to its last (precisely: to the longest first-to-last span of
any site after the site's first inventory, since all sites share one time axis).

There is **no manual standardization step**. FINN z-scales the predictors
internally when you fit (`env_autoscale = TRUE`, the default), stores the per-variable
mean/sd on the model, and re-applies them at prediction time. So you supply (and
keep) `env_dt` in natural units throughout.

## Resolve IDs across tables

Raw tables use names (`siteName`, `patchName`, `species_name`); FINN works with
integer indices aligned across all tables. `resolveSiteIDs()` does that alignment
and can build the initial cohorts in one pass. Pass the **processed** tree list
`obs_list$tree_dt` (not the raw one): `makeObsData()` returns it with the same
species lumping as `obs_dt`, so the two stay consistent.

```{r resolve-ids}
inputs <- resolveSiteIDs(
  tree_dt = obs_list$tree_dt,   # processed tree list (species lumped like obs_dt)
  obs_dt  = obs_dt,
  env_dt  = env_dt,
  createInitCohorts = TRUE
)
# obs_dt / env_dt now carry integer siteID/patchID/species, aligned across tables
head(inputs$obs_dt)
inputs$species_dt
```

`resolveSiteIDs()` also sets the time axis. A calendar year becomes the number
of years since the site's first inventory: the first inventory is year 0, the
initial state, and leaves `obs_dt`; the next two are observations at years 10
and 20. `env_dt` comes back with one row per site and year 1 to 20: the row of
year *k* drives the simulated year that ends *k* years after the first
inventory, so the inventory at year 10 is compared with the stand after ten
simulated years.

```{r time-axis}
unique(inputs$siteID_dt[, .(siteID, year, OrigYear)])[siteID == 1]
inputs$obs_dt[, .(rows = .N), by = .(year, period_length)]
inputs$env_dt[siteID == 1][c(1:2, .N)]
```

## Initial cohorts

With `createInitCohorts = TRUE`, `resolveSiteIDs()` returns a ready `CohortMat`
describing the starting state, the living trees of the first inventory; its
`dims` are `c(sites, patches, cohorts)`:

```{r init-cohorts}
init_cohorts <- inputs$initCohorts
init_cohorts
```

The list `resolveSiteIDs()` returns, initial cohorts included, can be saved with
`saveRDS()` and read back with `readRDS()`, so the preparation needs to run only
once. The cohorts keep a plain R copy of their arrays and rebuild their torch
tensors when `fit()`, `predict()` or `simulateForest()` use them:

```{r save-inputs}
path <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(inputs, path)
inputs_read <- readRDS(path)
# the cohorts read back hold the same trees
identical(inputs_read$initCohorts$dbh_r, init_cohorts$dbh_r)
```

To build cohorts yourself from a year-0 tree slice, call `makeInitCohorts()`
directly; it expects `siteID, patchID, species, dbh, treeName` (+ optional
`trees`):

```{r init-cohorts-manual, eval = FALSE}
init_trees <- inputs$tree_dt[living == TRUE & year == 0]
makeInitCohorts(init_trees, Nspecies = uniqueN(inputs$obs_dt$species))
```

## Checklist before fitting

- **One step is one year**: `year` counts years since the first inventory,
  `growth` and `mort` are yearly rates, and `period_length` gives each
  observation's interval in years. The intervals may differ between sites
  (FINN then scores every site over its own interval); `fix_period_length`
  keeps only sites with one interval length if you prefer a uniform design.
- **The initial state** (the first inventory) is used for `init_trees`, and the
  responses in `obs_dt` start *after* it.
- **`env_dt` covers every simulated year** from 1 to the last inventory, for
  every site; missing rows break the fit.
- **Units**: `dbh` in cm, `ba` in m², `reg` in trees ha⁻¹; env predictors in
  natural units (FINN standardizes them internally).
- **Species IDs** are contiguous integers `1..N`, aligned across tables; always
  go through `resolveSiteIDs()` rather than hand-coding them.

You now have `obs_dt`, `env_dt`, and `init_cohorts`, the inputs the
**Fitting FINN to forest inventory data** vignette consumes.

```{r session}
sessionInfo()
```
